---
const types = [
  ["Common", "A general name", "city, teacher, river"],
  ["Proper", "A specific name, usually capitalised", "London, Aisha, River Severn"],
  ["Collective", "A group named as one unit", "team, choir, flock"],
  ["Abstract", "An idea, quality, feeling or state", "courage, kindness, fear"]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Nouns - Naming words</h1>
  <p>
    A <strong>noun</strong> names a person, place, thing or idea. It can be the subject of a sentence or
    appear elsewhere in it.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>Types of noun</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Type</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Meaning</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Examples</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          types.map(([type, meaning, examples]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{type}</th>
              <td>{meaning}</td>
              <td>{examples}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p>
    In “The team of players arrived,” <strong>team</strong> is the collective noun and
    <strong>players</strong> is an ordinary plural. <em>City</em> is common; <strong>Gloucester</strong> is
    proper. Abstract nouns name things we can discuss but not usually hold, such as <strong>hope</strong> or
    <strong>pride</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A word’s job can change. “A <strong>run</strong> around the park was fun” uses <em>run</em> as a noun.
    “I <strong>run</strong> around the park” uses it as a verb. Decide from the sentence, not from the word
    alone.
  </p>
  <p class="try-it">
    <strong>Try it.</strong> In “Maya showed great patience,” find the proper noun and the abstract noun.
  </p>
  <p class="topic-lead">Tips and traps</p>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>Capitalise the specific name</h2>
      <p>
        We swam in the river.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
        A common noun stays lower case.
      </p>
      <p>
        We swam in the River Severn.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
        The name of one river is proper.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>A plural is not always collective</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Players</strong> names more than one person. <strong>Team</strong> names the group as one unit.
      </p>
      <p>The team is ready. <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span> The group acts as one.</p>
      <p>
        The team are wearing their shirts.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
        British English can use a plural verb when you mean the members.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
